IF enqueue AND NOT prev_enqueue THEN
    IF elementCount < capacity THEN
        THIS^.Enqueue(buffer, item, tail, elementCount);
        status := 16#0000;
    ELSE
        status := 16#8002; // Buffer full
    END_IF
END_IF

// Rising edge detection for dequeue
IF dequeue AND NOT prev_dequeue THEN
    IF elementCount > 0 THEN
        THIS^.Dequeue(buffer, item, head, elementCount);
        status := 16#0000;
    ELSE
        status := 16#8001; // Buffer empty
    END_IF
END_IF

// Update edge tracking
prev_enqueue := enqueue;
prev_dequeue := dequeue;
```

The reset clears all indices and forces reinitialization on the next cycle. Enqueue operations add to the tail while dequeue removes from the head, both tracked by elementCount and guarded by capacity checks that set appropriate status codes. _enqueue := enqueue;
prev_dequeue := dequeue;

The implementation uses a circular buffer where head marks the dequeue position and tail marks the enqueue position. On enqueue, elements are added at the tail and it wraps around modulo capacity. On dequeue, elements are read from the head position. The method retrieves the buffer capacity using the ADR operator to pass the array reference and calls the system function to determine its size. The Dequeue method removes an element by reading from the head position and shifting the head forward. It retrieves the element's address within the buffer using the offset calculation (head * elementSize), then copies the data out using MemMove. After extracting the value, it clears the original position by writing zeros, advances the head pointer circularly with modulo arithmetic, and decrements the element count. Finally, it updates the head position back to the instance memory. The Enqueue method then takes over, preparing to store the new item at the tail position. The implementation appears sound overall. One consideration is whether the capacity calculation should happen once or be recalculated each cycle—doing it once per invocation works fine given the buffer remains fixed per instance. I should also ensure type safety since the VARIANT pointer needs to handle arbitrary data types correctly.

The approach of storing the buffer address in capacity is appropriate, allowing GetBufferCapacity to derive the actual capacity on the first call and cache it. For the implementation itself, I'll create helper methods within the function block to handle the queue operations cleanly.

The main body checks for reset first, then processes rising edges on the enqueue and dequeue signals. When enqueuing, it verifies there's room and calls the internal Enqueue routine; when dequeuing, it confirms the queue isn't empty before calling Dequeue. Each operation updates the previous signal state and sets the appropriate status code if an error occurs.

Helper methods handle the actual data movement. GetBufferInfo retrieves capacity and element size using system functions. Enqueue calculates the target address in the circular buffer using the current tail position, copies the new item there, advances the tail, and increments the element count. Dequeue similarly locates the head position, moves the front element out, clears the original location, and updates both head position and element count.

I should consider how the implementation handles edge cases like invalid buffer pointers or mismatched types, but the VARIANT type should manage these appropriately. The MemMove function should work reliably for copying data. The circular buffer logic appears sound across different scenarios—when full, enqueue operations are rejected; when empty, dequeue operations are rejected; and the head/tail indices wrap correctly as elements are added and removed. This implementation should be ready to finalize.

